## ACT 1: The GPU Utilization Trap (00:00 – 06:30)

**Q1: What is the "Blackwell Paradox"? Why does chasing throughput kill p99 latency?**

Simple explanation:
Companies want their GPUs busy almost all the time — that saves money. So teams use tricks like bigger batch sizes (processing many requests together) and speculative decoding (guessing ahead to go faster). These tricks make the *average* response fast. But here's the catch: if one important request gets stuck in a big batch, it has to wait for the whole batch to finish. So while most requests are fast, a few critical ones become very slow — sometimes dangerously slow. That "worst case" number is called p99 latency, and it's the one that actually causes outages, not the average.

Simple line you can say out loud:
*"Making the average faster can make the worst case much worse — and in production, the worst case is the one that breaks things."*

---

**Q2: If you cap GPU usage at 85% to protect latency, how do you justify the idle 15% to a CFO?**

Simple explanation:
Leaving some GPU capacity unused looks like wasted money on a spreadsheet. But the real comparison isn't "idle capacity vs. money saved" — it's "idle capacity vs. cost of an outage." If you push GPUs too hard and a critical transaction times out, you can face SLA penalty payments, failed transactions, and a chain reaction of failures across other systems. Once you put a real dollar number on what an outage costs, the small idle percentage looks cheap by comparison.

Simple line you can say out loud:
*"Idle capacity has a visible cost. An outage has a bigger, hidden one — you just don't see it until it happens."*

